<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fu Youde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1394">1394</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fu Youde was a general of the founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>. In 1371 he led one of the armies that conquered the Ming Xia state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, and in the following years he campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in the north. In 1381 he commanded the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> from the last Yuan loyalists. Ennobled as Duke of Ying, he was forced to commit suicide in 1394 during the emperor's purges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fu-youde.jpg" alt="Fu Youde" width="320" height="583" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous (Category:Ming dynasty)Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ming_Dynasty_Portrait_of_Fu_Youde.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fu Youde was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>, in northern Anhui, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>; the date of his birth is unknown. His family fled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> floods and the unrest that followed them, settling first in Dangshan and later in Yingzhou. Of humble rural origins and without formal education, he made his name locally as a young man for leadership and martial skill. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban Rebellion</a> broke out in 1351 he joined a rebel band in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuzhou">Xuzhou</a>.</p><p>He served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-liner">Han Lin'er</a> under Li Xixi, one of the generals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-futong">Liu Futong</a>, until defeats drove that army into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> in 1358. He then followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-yuzhen">Ming Yuzhen</a>, a general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-shouhui">Xu Shouhui</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-youliang">Chen Youliang</a> killed Xu in 1360 he passed to Chen's state of Han. Unrewarded there, he went over with his troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a>, the future Hongwu Emperor, in September 1361.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under Zhu, Fu was valued as a tactician who led from the front. Serving under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-da">Xu Da</a> and Chang Yuchun, he fought in the victories over Chen Han in 1363–64, over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-shicheng">Zhang Shicheng</a>'s state of Wu in 1366–67 and in the conquest of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1368–1370. In November 1370 he was made Marquis of Yingchuan. In January 1371, with Tang He's army and Liao Yongzhong's fleet, he struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> from the north while the main attack was expected along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>.</p><p>Between campaigns he built fortifications on the northern frontier and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and taught the emperor's sons the art of war. In 1381 he led 300,000 soldiers into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, with Lan Yu and Mu Ying as deputies, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a> by January 1382 and Dali in April. He set up a provincial administration and returned only in April 1384. He later served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, fought rebels in Yunnan again and was Feng Sheng's deputy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> campaign of 1387 against the Mongol chieftain Naghachu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In March 1392 the emperor angrily refused his request for nearly 1,000 mu of land, the only recorded rebuke of his career. After Lan Yu was executed for treason in March 1393 and thousands of his associates purged, Fu died by suicide on 20 December 1394, and the generals Wang Bi and Feng Sheng died within two months. Historians see the three deaths as the emperor's work. In 1645 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongguang-emperor">Hongguang Emperor</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a> gave Fu the posthumous name Wujing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fu Youde” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1371 Ming Empire armies under Tang He and <b>Fu Youde</b> advanced by river and over the mountains; Chongqing surrendered, and Ming Sheng was sent into exile in Goryeo.</blockquote><a href="/state/ming-xia">Ming Xia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Suzhou, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.63,116.97&amp;year=1384" aria-label="Suzhou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7536 944 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Fu Youde"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7986 1214h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7986 1214h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.63,116.97&amp;year=1384">What was here in 1384? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
